    <article class="log-article prose" aria-label="Engineering log #7">
      <p>The answer is <strong>yes, support WebAssembly</strong>—but do not turn five backends into five divergent definitions of Oreslang. A common language contract and a query-based compilation engine are more fundamental than adding another code generator.</p>

      <section class="log-section" id="inventory">
        <h2>What exists today: five efforts, different levels of maturity</h2>
        <p>An October 14, 2026 repository review found the following. These are descriptions of the documented state, <em>not</em> claims of feature parity:</p>
        <div class="log-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Oreslang compiler repositories" tabindex="0">
          <table><thead><tr><th>Repository</th><th>Current role</th><th>Documented maturity</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><code>oreslang-source.java</code></td><td>Java / GraalVM / Truffle reference implementation</td><td>Documents file-granular incremental compilation; a larger language and runtime implementation than the experimental backends</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>oreslang-source.llvm</code></td><td>Native compiler and runtime</td><td>Experimental source-to-LLVM subset; major actor, async and heap features remain unsupported</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>oreslang-source.net</code></td><td>Windows-first .NET Native AOT</td><td>Scaffold, not a full compiler</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>oreslang-source.forge</code></td><td>Independent C++20 compiler and runtime experiment</td><td>Bootstrap parser/IR; minimal native machine-code generation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>oreslang-wasm</code></td><td>Wasm runtime ABI, host integration and LLVM bridge</td><td>Runnable proof of concept; no complete Oreslang-to-Wasm compiler</td></tr>
          </tbody></table>
        </div>
        <p>These descriptions are an engineering snapshot drawn from repository documentation, not independent verification of every advertised feature. Some of the source repositories are private and their READMEs are not accessible to all site visitors.</p>
        <p>Two additional repositories clarify the plan. <code>oreslang-source.wasm</code> exists but was empty at the time of review; <code>oreslang-common-source</code> already contains architecture and IR proposals but no working shared semantic compiler. The latter is the right home for our future common HIR → MIR layer and incremental query engine.</p>
        <p>Keep <code>oreslang-wasm</code> responsible for ABI and runtime integration, and eventually use <code>oreslang-source.wasm</code> as the compiler adapter. Keep Forge an independent experimental native compiler unless a tested migration makes it genuinely shared infrastructure. Existing repository visibility and implementation maturity must not be confused with support guarantees.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="wasm">
        <h2>Wasm belongs in the architecture—but is not a magic sandbox</h2>
        <p>The near-term compilation route is verified Oreslang MIR → LLVM lowering → wasm32 object/module, with a versioned runtime ABI. Later, support component-model interfaces through WIT and explicitly pinned WASI host profiles. A direct MIR → Wasm backend should be considered only if the added maintenance cost is justified by proven advantages over LLVM's Wasm target.</p>
        <figure class="log7-figure">
          <img src="/logs/7/wasm-profiles.svg" alt="A verified Oreslang Wasm module deploys either inside a browser Worker or inside a WASI server or edge host, with independent import, capability and resource controls." width="1100" height="360" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption>Two portable Wasm deployment families, with different host obligations.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Browser deployment needs explicit Web Worker termination, bounded memories and tables, host-controlled capabilities, and a real asynchronous actor transport. Server and edge deployment additionally needs versioned WASI support and carefully defined resource admission. Wasm linear memory is not, on its own, a complete untrusted-code isolation system: CPU budgets, memory ceilings, imported functions, and host policy still matter.</p>
        <p>More target environments should be handled through the existing <code>oreslang-target-profiles</code> registry—browser, edge, iOS, Android, and embedded—rather than implying that compiling Wasm makes every device compatible. Mobile app integration, native FFI, hardware access, and MCU constraints remain distinct runtime responsibilities.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="architecture">
        <h2>The architectural center: verified HIR/MIR plus an incremental query engine</h2>
        <p>Share language meaning, not backend object representations. The frontend produces typed high-level IR (HIR); lowering produces verified mid-level IR (MIR) with explicit control flow, ownership transfers, cleanup and effects. Backend adapters own platform layout, code emission and runtime bindings.</p>
        <figure class="log7-figure">
          <img src="/logs/7/compiler-architecture.svg" alt="Oreslang sources pass through a proposed shared incremental semantic core, then a persistent query cache, and finally five adapters: Java GraalVM, .NET Native AOT, LLVM, Forge, and Wasm." width="1100" height="630" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption>Proposed end state. The shared semantic engine and full cross-backend integration are not implemented yet.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Do not require all compilers to switch overnight. Java/GraalVM and LLVM should keep their existing independent parsing and borrow-checking paths initially. Introduce a versioned HIR/MIR contract, independent adapters, and executable conformance fixtures before replacing proven semantics. <code>oreslang-common-source</code> can gradually take over shared analysis after demonstrated equivalence.</p>
        <p>The common IR must not quietly inherit LLVM integer widths, JVM object models, undefined overflow, or unsupported source constructs. Every backend should explicitly reject unsupported operations and declare the features and ABI contracts it implements.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="incrementality">
        <h2>What “total incremental compilation” should mean</h2>
        <p>The goal is <strong>incremental reuse at every compiler stage</strong>—not the impossible promise that every source edit has zero downstream work. Files, declarations, functions and specialization instances should be independently cached whenever their semantics permit it.</p>
        <pre><code>source_text(file)
  → tokens(file)
  → syntax_tree(file)
  → symbols(module)
  → typed_hir(function)
  → borrow_proof(function)
  → effect_summary(function)
  → verified_mir(function)
  → optimize(function, target)
  → codegen(function, target)
  → link(artifact, target)</code></pre>
        <p>Important distinctions: incremental lexing/parsing can reuse unchanged syntax regions; type checking should depend on symbol and contract changes, not merely file timestamps; MIR optimization and code generation should use function- and specialization-granular artifacts; final AOT linking may retain larger unavoidable work.</p>
        <p>Java already documents file-level incremental compilation. The next improvement is function-level semantic and code generation invalidation, then sharing target-independent work among backends that consume the same verified representation.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="invalidations">
        <h2>Track exactly what changed, and exactly who depends on it</h2>
        <p>Consider an existing Oreslang function:</p>
        <pre><code>pub fnc calculate(int x) : int &#123;
    return x * 2;
&#125;</code></pre>
        <p>Changing <code>x * 2</code> to <code>x * 3</code> changes the function body, not the public signature. Usually its callers do not need to be type-checked again. But the function must be regenerated, and callers embedding its machine code through inlining might need code regeneration.</p>
        <p>Changing <code>: int</code> to <code>: string</code>, altering generic constraints, or changing an inferred effect or actor capability can invalidate callers' semantic results.</p>
        <figure class="log7-figure">
          <img src="/logs/7/incremental-invalidation.svg" alt="A body-only edit recompiles the function and body-dependent inlining, while a public contract edit propagates type and effect checks to affected dependents." width="1100" height="475" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption>Different change types have different invalidation boundaries.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>In addition to dependencies on definitions, track dependencies on <em>absence</em>. Adding an overload, trait implementation, symbol, or candidate import can change resolution even when none of the previously referenced declarations changed. A correct engine must invalidate those negative-lookups too.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="query-model">
        <h2>Versioned, persistent, content-addressed queries</h2>
        <p>Queries that read mutable external state or use nondeterministic inputs must declare those inputs or bypass caching; otherwise, a red/green dependency graph cannot soundly reuse their results.</p>
        <p>Each query result should record its key, actual dependencies and semantic fingerprint. Store immutable results in a content-addressed cache. Validate inputs using a red/green strategy: if an input changed but a query's result is semantically unchanged, dependent results may remain green.</p>
        <pre><code>QueryKey = (
  operation,
  stable_symbol_id,
  relevant_inputs,
  dependency_fingerprints,
  semantic_compiler_version,
  enabled_features,
  target_profile_if_relevant,
  ABI_and_toolchain_versions_if_relevant
)</code></pre>
        <p>Do <em>not</em> put a target triple into the keys for truly target-independent parsing, typing or MIR. Do include target data layout, runtime ABI, toolchain identity, optimization settings and environment grants where those facts affect results. A cache collision or stale schema must never turn invalid ownership proofs into executable code.</p>
        <p>Use stable IDs that survive unrelated edits; deterministic serialization; atomic cache publication; checksummed and size-bounded artifacts; bounded eviction; and compiler-schema versioning. Remote caches need trust boundaries, authentication, and provenance verification. Do not treat cached files as inherently safe because they came from CI.</p>
        <p>This architecture draws from <a href="https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/queries/incremental-compilation-in-detail.html">rustc's incremental compilation design</a> and <a href="https://github.com/salsa-rs/salsa">Salsa's incremental query model</a>, without committing prematurely to their implementation language or dependency architecture.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="oreslang-specific">
        <h2>Oreslang's special incremental correctness hazards</h2>
        <p><strong>Borrowing and isolation:</strong> cached proofs must depend on complete control-flow semantics, type facts, alias analysis, ownership contracts, and relevant runtime rules. A compiler upgrade or a change affecting a live borrow across <code>await</code>, <code>select</code>, actor send, or cleanup can invalidate a proof even if the textual function body looks unchanged.</p>
        <p><strong>Actors and effects:</strong> actor message sendability, capabilities, error effects, mailbox contracts, and cancellation behavior are part of the semantic dependency graph. Target-profile admission is independent of runtime capability grants; each side must enforce its own obligations.</p>
        <p><strong>Generic specialization:</strong> cache instantiated functions by a canonical identity including resolved type arguments, relevant trait implementations and any target-dependent layout. Adding a competing implementation can change an earlier resolution.</p>
        <p><strong>Hot loading:</strong> use stable code-unit identity plus generation-specific versions and compatibility manifests. Reuse unaffected code across generations, but reject layout, ABI, capability or protocol incompatibilities. Hot reload never implies an unsafe in-place mutation of active actor state.</p>
        <p><strong>Compiler-generated safepoints:</strong> scheduling guards and watchdog checks must survive optimization. An incremental optimizer must not reuse a version that omitted newly required cooperation points.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="aot">
        <h2>Incremental compilation does not automatically imply incremental final AOT images</h2>
        <p>Separate the <em>language compiler's incremental work</em> from the <em>platform toolchain's link and image build</em>. Function-level MIR and object caches can be effective for LLVM and Wasm. GraalVM Native Image and .NET Native AOT may still perform substantial whole-program work for reachability, trimming, specialization or final linking.</p>
        <p>Our requirement should be to measure and minimize each layer independently: time to diagnostics, time to verified MIR, time to per-function object/assembly emission, and time to a runnable binary. For .NET, strict Native AOT remains distinct from targeting CIL. For Java/Graal, genuine guest program AOT must be demonstrated rather than inferred from the fact that the host runtime can be compiled ahead of time.</p>
        <p>Where whole-program optimization requires a global recompute, report that cost. Prefer sound incremental work with transparent limitations over a fast cache that silently changes observable behavior.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="verification">
        <h2>Correctness first: clean builds are the oracle</h2>
        <p>For release verification, every incremental build must be checked against a clean build for the same source, compiler versions, configuration and target. Admission outcomes and observable execution must agree. For artifact formats promising reproducibility, output hashes should agree as well.</p>
        <pre><code>incremental_compile(source, target)
  ≡ clean_compile(source, target)

Verify:
  accepted / rejected programs
  typed and capability diagnostics
  observable values, effects, exceptions
  ownership and actor isolation
  stable artifact hashes where promised</code></pre>
        <p>Build a change matrix: no-op rebuild; one-character whitespace edit; private body edit; exported signature edit; changed implicit effects; generic constraint edit; added overload; deleted type; actor protocol change; hot-load generation bump; compiler upgrade; corrupt cache; and concurrent build publication. Include negative cases where compiler crashes, missing tools and timeouts are infrastructure failures—not successful feature rejection.</p>
        <p>Use <code>oreslang-platform-conformance</code> to collect exact-source, exact-binary evidence. Compare real Java and LLVM compiler runs on supported shared language subsets before claiming backend parity; extend to Wasm, .NET and Forge only as executable adapters exist.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="roadmap">
        <h2>Implementation order: evolve, do not rewrite everything</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Specification:</strong> commit typed, versioned HIR/MIR fixtures, stable symbol identities, numeric semantics, diagnostics and a mandatory verifier into <code>oreslang-common-source</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Incremental engine:</strong> prototype declaration-level queries, semantic fingerprints, reverse dependency graphs, absence dependencies and persistent cache validation. Evaluate in-process Rust/Salsa, native C++ and service-based packaging rather than declaring a winner without measurements.</li>
          <li><strong>First vertical slice:</strong> parse and compile literals, arithmetic, locals, calls and branches through real Java and LLVM adapter paths; compare clean and incremental outcomes.</li>
          <li><strong>Expand semantic proofs:</strong> generics, types, borrows, actor sendability, async, effects, capabilities and exception cleanup; migrate only verified common implementations.</li>
          <li><strong>Integrate backends:</strong> add verified Wasm lowering from the native path, then independent .NET and Forge consumption of shared contracts as readiness allows.</li>
          <li><strong>Optimize the expensive tail:</strong> per-function object caches, incremental linking where available, AOT pipeline benchmarks, authenticated remote caching and language-server latency.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Keep each backend releasable while the common architecture matures. Pin immutable common-contract revisions and provide rollback paths. A shared repository is not proof that a shared compiler works.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="log-section" id="outcome">
        <h2>The real definition of success</h2>
        <p>The ambition is simple: <strong>one Oreslang language, five production-ready execution strategies eventually, and no needless recompilation.</strong> Progress should be expressed as verified semantic coverage and measured invalidation boundaries—not repository count or cache-hit percentages in isolation.</p>
        <p>For a function-body edit, we want unchanged interface dependents to stay valid, affected code and inlined callers to regenerate correctly, and the resulting program to behave exactly like a clean build. For a contract change, we want precise, transitive invalidation. For every actor and borrow proof, we want fail-closed correctness.</p>
        <p>That is the path toward genuinely incremental Oreslang compilation without sacrificing the two defining goals of the language: predictable concurrency and explicit isolation.</p>
      </section>
